/* =====================================================================
   PROLACTINOMA (Lactotroph adenoma / PitNET) — QSP Mechanistic Map
   including drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia, stalk-effect
   hyperprolactinaemia and macroprolactinaemia as the same equations
   evaluated at different points
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE ORGANISING IDEA

     PROLACTINOMA IS THE ONE PITUITARY TUMOUR WHOSE GROWTH IS UNDER
     TONIC INHIBITORY NEUROENDOCRINE CONTROL — SO A RECEPTOR AGONIST IS
     NOT MERELY ANTI-SECRETORY, IT IS ANTI-TUMORAL.

     AND IT IS THE ONE ENDOCRINE DISEASE IN WHICH THE NUMBER YOU
     MEASURE AND THE SIGNAL THE BODY SEES CAN DIVERGE BY TWO ORDERS OF
     MAGNITUDE IN EITHER DIRECTION.

   Every other anterior-pituitary cell type is driven by a releasing
   hormone; the lactotroph is instead held down by one — dopamine
   arriving through the portal vessels from tuberoinfundibular (TIDA)
   neurones. Remove the dopamine and prolactin rises; add a
   higher-affinity agonist and prolactin falls. Uniquely, the same
   receptor also governs whether the cell divides and whether it stays
   alive. That is why prolactinoma is the only pituitary tumour for
   which drugs — not surgery — are first-line therapy anywhere in the
   world.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   ONE RECEPTOR, THREE TIME CONSTANTS  (clusters 3-5)

   D2 occupancy on the lactotroph produces three separable effects with
   three separable time constants and three separable potencies. This is
   the single most useful quantitative structure on this map:

     (1) EXOCYTOSIS BLOCK      minutes-hours   most potent
         Gi/o -> GIRK K+ efflux -> hyperpolarisation -> voltage-gated
         Ca2+ channels close -> [Ca2+]i falls -> granule fusion stops.
         Consequence: prolactin falls 50-80% within HOURS of the first
         cabergoline dose, before anything else has happened.

     (2) TRANSCRIPTION BLOCK   days-weeks      intermediate
         cAMP/PKA down, ERK1/2 down -> Pit-1 / ERalpha drive on the PRL
         gene falls -> mRNA falls -> granule stores deplete.
         Consequence: the plateau of biochemical response at 4-8 weeks.

     (3) ANTIPROLIFERATIVE /   weeks-months    least potent
         CYTOREDUCTIVE
         PI3K/Akt and MAPK withdrawal, p27 up, cyclin D down, TGF-beta1
         up, caspase-3 apoptosis; and separately a rapid loss of
         CYTOPLASMIC VOLUME (granule depletion, RER involution).
         Consequence: tumour shrinkage lags biochemical control by
         months, and DOSE ESCALATION FOR A NON-SHRINKING MACROADENOMA
         WHOSE PROLACTIN IS ALREADY NORMAL IS RATIONAL — the three
         effects sit on three different points of the occupancy axis.

   The cytoreduction itself is drawn as TWO processes because they
   reverse on completely different timescales:

     CELL VOLUME (fast, reversible)  -- shrinks ~50% in 4-12 weeks and
        RE-EXPANDS within weeks of withdrawal. This is why a giant
        prolactinoma can decompress the chiasm in 72 HOURS, and why
        stopping the drug can restore a mass effect that had gone.
     CELL NUMBER (slow, not reversible) -- apoptotic loss over months
        to years. This is the only quantity whose loss can be banked,
        and it is what makes drug withdrawal possible at all.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE CONTROLLER IS ALREADY SATURATED AT DIAGNOSIS  (clusters 1-2)

   Prolactin feeds back on its own secretion through the SHORT LOOP:
   PRL -> PRLR/JAK2/STAT5 on TIDA neurones -> tyrosine hydroxylase up ->
   more portal dopamine. In a prolactinoma this loop is intact — the
   transmitter is working perfectly. What has failed is the RECEIVER:
   tumour lactotrophs carry reduced D2 receptor density (and a shifted
   D2S:D2L short/long isoform ratio).

   Two consequences are drawn on this map and both are generated by the
   equations rather than asserted:

     * At diagnosis, TIDA drive and portal dopamine are ALREADY MAXIMAL.
       There is no endogenous reserve left to recruit. Therefore the
       only way to add D2 signal is a ligand of far higher affinity than
       dopamine itself: cabergoline Ki ~0.5-1 nM against dopamine's
       micromolar affinity for the low-affinity receptor state. THE
       DRUGS WORK BY OUT-COMPETING A NEUROTRANSMITTER THAT IS ALREADY
       PRESENT AT ITS CEILING.

     * Because the feedback saturates, the loop has SPARE CAPACITY only
       below a critical lactotroph mass. That saturation is what makes
       drug withdrawal a THRESHOLD phenomenon rather than a graded one,
       and it is why a visible tumour remnant on MRI predicts relapse.
       Remission is the drug handing control back to the hypothalamus,
       and it holds only if the mass handed back is small enough for
       endogenous tone to hold.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   ONE OCCUPANCY EQUATION, THREE DIRECTIONS  (clusters 3, 16-18)

   Net D2 signal is written once, as a competition among every ligand
   present, each weighted by its own intrinsic activity:

     SIGNAL = (D2R density) x SUM_i [ e_i * (C_i/Ki_i) ] / (1 + SUM_j C_j/Kj_j)

   with e = 1.0 for dopamine, quinagolide and cabergoline, ~0.8 for
   bromocriptine (partial), ~0.25 for aripiprazole (partial) and 0 for
   risperidone/paliperidone/amisulpride/haloperidol. From that one line
   the map derives, without any additional rule:

     * dopamine agonist            -> SIGNAL up   -> PRL falls
     * D2 antagonist antipsychotic -> displaces dopamine with e=0
                                   -> SIGNAL down -> PRL RISES
     * aripiprazole alone          -> displaces dopamine (e 1.0 -> 0.25)
                                   -> SIGNAL modestly down, mild rise
     * aripiprazole ADDED to risperidone -> displaces an e=0 ligand with
                                   an e=0.25 ligand -> SIGNAL UP -> PRL
                                   FALLS. The documented add-on effect
                                   is an arithmetic consequence, not a
                                   separate mechanism.

   The same equation with the DELIVERY term cut instead of the receptor
   term gives stalk-effect (disconnection) hyperprolactinaemia — and
   gives it a CEILING, because only the finite normal lactotroph pool is
   disinhibited and its feedback still works. That is the origin of the
   bedside rule "prolactin 60 with a 3 cm sellar mass is stalk effect,
   not prolactinoma": on this map the ceiling is computed, not stated.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   THE MEASUREMENT LAYER IS PART OF THE PATHOPHYSIOLOGY  (cluster 9)

   Three of the classic management errors in this disease are not
   biological failures at all. They are failures of the map from
   BIOACTIVE prolactin to REPORTED prolactin, so that map is drawn
   explicitly as its own cluster with its own equations:

     HOOK EFFECT (falsely LOW). A two-site sandwich immunoassay needs
       one analyte molecule to bridge capture and label antibody. In
       vast antigen excess every antibody is saturated singly, bridges
       fail, and signal FALLS. A giant prolactinoma with a true
       prolactin of 20 000 ng/mL can report 45. The 1:100 dilution that
       recovers it is the same curve evaluated 100-fold down the
       analyte axis — exactly the structure that makes excess heparin
       abolish the serotonin-release assay.

     MACROPROLACTINAEMIA (falsely HIGH). Prolactin-IgG complexes
       (big-big prolactin, ~150 kDa) are cleared slowly, so they
       accumulate; they are immunoreactive but cannot reach the
       extravascular receptor. Measured prolactin is high, BIOACTIVE
       prolactin is normal, the gonadal axis is intact — and treatment
       is futile. PEG precipitation recovery <40% is the discriminator
       and is computed here from the same two pools.

     GLYCOSYLATED AND CLEAVED FORMS. G-PRL has reduced bioactivity;
       the 16 kDa cathepsin-D fragment is anti-angiogenic and is a
       different molecule pharmacologically.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   SAFETY IS A SELECTIVITY RATIO, NOT A DRUG PROPERTY  (cluster 17)

   Cabergoline's adverse effects are drawn as occupancies of OTHER
   receptors computed from the SAME plasma concentration:

     D2 (area postrema, outside the BBB)  -> nausea/vomiting, with an
        explicit tolerance state — which is the whole reason for
        bedtime dosing and slow titration.
     alpha-1 / D2 (peripheral vascular)   -> first-dose orthostatic
        hypotension.
     5-HT2B (valve interstitial cells)    -> ergot valvulopathy. Risk is
        drawn as CUMULATIVE occupancy, which is why the Parkinson's
        literature (3-4 mg/DAY) and the prolactinoma literature (0.5-2
        mg/WEEK, a 10-40x lower exposure) reach opposite conclusions
        from the same drug. On this map the discordance is a dose scale,
        not a controversy.
     D3 (mesolimbic)                      -> impulse control disorders:
        the one adverse effect that does NOT come from the ergot
        skeleton and therefore does not spare quinagolide.
     D2 (mesolimbic, in a patient on antipsychotics) -> psychosis
        relapse: the reason drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia is
        generally NOT treated with a dopamine agonist.

   A sweep of the 5-HT2B/D2 selectivity ratio over four decades leaves
   efficacy flat and collapses valvular risk — the same result the
   P2X3-selectivity sweep gives in the chronic cough model, arrived at
   from a completely different receptor pair.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   TWO DRIVERS, NOT ONE: OESTROGEN  (cluster 7)

   Oestradiol is a lactotroph mitogen and a direct transcriptional
   activator of the PRL gene through ERalpha at the distal enhancer, and
   it down-regulates D2R. Pregnancy therefore does three bad things at
   once (drug stopped + oestrogen 100x + placental lactogen) and one
   good one (post-partum involution, occasionally curative). The
   observed ~2.7% vs ~23% symptomatic enlargement risk for micro- vs
   macroadenoma in pregnancy is a mass-and-geometry consequence on this
   map, not a tabulated number.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   READING THE MAP

     bold red        the autocatalytic / self-worsening arms
     bold blue       dopaminergic control and the drugs that use it
     dashed          measurement, not biology
     diamond         clinical endpoint or decision
     hexagon         drug or intervention
     octagon         irreversible state (banked damage)
     grey            differential diagnosis / non-tumoral causes

   ===================================================================== */

digraph prolactinoma_qsp {
    graph [rankdir=TB, newrank=true, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, labelloc="t", nodesep=0.22,
           ranksep=0.45, compound=true, splines=spline, overlap=false,
           bgcolor="white",
           label=<<b>PROLACTINOMA — Quantitative Systems Pharmacology Mechanistic Map</b><br/>
<i>Lactotroph PitNET · dopamine D2 control of secretion AND growth · the measurement layer as pathophysiology</i><br/>
<font point-size="10">One D2 occupancy equation generates dopamine-agonist efficacy, antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinaemia, the aripiprazole add-on paradox and stalk-effect disconnection<br/>
Three time constants from one receptor: exocytosis (hours) · transcription (weeks) · cytoreduction (months) — split into reversible cell VOLUME and bankable cell NUMBER<br/>
Bioactive vs reported prolactin: hook effect (falsely low), macroprolactinaemia (falsely high), PEG recovery · Safety as a 5-HT2B/D2/D3 selectivity ratio</font>>];

    node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, shape=box, style="rounded,filled",
          fillcolor="white", color="gray40", margin="0.06,0.03"];
    edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, color="gray45", arrowsize=0.65];

    /* ============ 1. HYPOTHALAMIC DOPAMINERGIC CONTROL ============ */
    subgraph cluster_tida {
        label = <<b>1 · Hypothalamic control — the lactotroph is INHIBITED, not driven</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#e8f0fb"; color="#3b6fb5"; fontcolor="#1f4a8a";
        ARC          [label="Arcuate nucleus\ndorsomedial ARC"];
        TIDA         [label="TIDA neurone\ntuberoinfundibular dopaminergic\n(A12 group)", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        TH           [label="Tyrosine hydroxylase\n(TH) — rate limiting", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        DASYN        [label="Dopamine synthesis\nL-DOPA → DA"];
        VMAT         [label="VMAT2 vesicular\npackaging"];
        DAREL        [label="DA release into\nmedian eminence", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        THDA         [label="THDA / PHDA neurones\n(periventricular, accessory)"];
        DAT          [label="DAT reuptake\n+ COMT/MAO degradation"];
        STAT5T       [label="STAT5b in TIDA\n(short-loop sensor)", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        PRLRT        [label="PRLR on TIDA\ndendrites"];
        SHORTLOOP    [label="SHORT-LOOP FEEDBACK\nPRL → TH↑ → more DA", fillcolor="#f9d7d7", color="#b52b2b", penwidth=2];
        TRHN         [label="TRH neurone (PVN)\nPRL-releasing factor"];
        VIPN         [label="VIP / PACAP\nPRL-releasing"];
        OXTN         [label="Oxytocin neurone\nPRL-releasing"];
        SEROT        [label="5-HT (dorsal raphe)\n→ PRL release"];
        GABAN        [label="GABA / GAL / opioid\n(μ-opioid → TIDA inhibition)"];
        SUCK         [label="Suckling / nipple\nafferent reflex"];
        STRESS       [label="Stress (5-HT, opioid)\nacute PRL rise"];
        SLEEPP       [label="Sleep-entrained\nnocturnal PRL surge"];
    }

    /* ============ 2. PORTAL DELIVERY AND THE STALK ============ */
    subgraph cluster_portal {
        label = <<b>2 · Delivery: median eminence → portal vessels → lactotroph (the term a stalk lesion cuts)</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eaf3fb"; color="#3b6fb5"; fontcolor="#1f4a8a";
        ME           [label="Median eminence\nfenestrated capillaries"];
        PORTAL       [label="Hypophyseal portal\nvessels — long + short", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        DAPORT       [label="PORTAL DA concentration\n(~2-10 nM at pituitary)", fillcolor="#cfe0f7", penwidth=2];
        STALK        [label="Pituitary stalk\npatency", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        STALKC       [label="STALK COMPRESSION\n(non-functioning macroadenoma,\ncraniopharyngioma, RCC, trauma)", fillcolor="#e6e6e6", color="gray30"];
        DISCONN      [label="DISCONNECTION\nhyperprolactinaemia\n— CEILING ~25-100 ng/mL", shape=octagon, fillcolor="#e6e6e6", color="gray20"];
        SYSDA        [label="Systemic DA does NOT\nreach the pituitary\n(BBB-excluded, portal-delivered)", shape=note, fillcolor="#fbfbe8"];
    }

    /* ============ 3. LACTOTROPH D2 RECEPTOR SIGNALLING ============ */
    subgraph cluster_d2 {
        label = <<b>3 · Lactotroph D2 receptor — ONE occupancy equation, THREE downstream branches</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#dfeaf9"; color="#26558f"; fontcolor="#12386a";
        D2R          [label="D2 receptor DENSITY\non lactotroph\n(REDUCED in tumour = resistance)", fillcolor="#b9d3f2", penwidth=2];
        D2S          [label="D2S short isoform\n(preferentially couples\nto antiproliferation)"];
        D2L          [label="D2L long isoform\nD2S:D2L ratio shifted\nin resistant tumours"];
        OCCUP        [label="NET D2 SIGNAL\nΣ eᵢ·(Cᵢ/Kᵢ) / (1+Σ Cⱼ/Kⱼ)\n× D2R density", shape=doubleoctagon, fillcolor="#9dc2ee", color="#12386a", penwidth=2.4];
        GIO          [label="Gαi/o coupling"];
        AC           [label="Adenylate cyclase ↓"];
        CAMP         [label="cAMP ↓ → PKA ↓", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        GIRK         [label="GIRK (Kir3) K⁺ efflux\n→ hyperpolarisation", fillcolor="#cfe0f7"];
        VGCC         [label="L-type Ca²⁺ channel\nclosure"];
        CAI          [label="[Ca²⁺]ᵢ ↓", fillcolor="#cfe0f7", penwidth=2];
        ERKL         [label="ERK1/2 ↓"];
        AKTL         [label="PI3K / Akt / mTOR ↓"];
        P27          [label="p27^Kip1 ↑ ·\ncyclin D1 ↓"];
        TGFB         [label="TGF-β1 ↑\n(autocrine antiproliferative)"];
        CASP         [label="Caspase-3 activation\n→ apoptosis", fillcolor="#f9d7d7"];
        DRD2V        [label="DRD2 polymorphisms /\nreduced mRNA · GRK/β-arrestin\nbias", fillcolor="#f2e6f7"];
        FILAMIN      [label="Filamin A loss\n→ D2R mis-trafficking\n(resistance mechanism)", fillcolor="#f2e6f7"];
        NEUROD       [label="D2R desensitisation\n(β-arrestin2, GRK2)"];
    }

    /* ============ 4. PROLACTIN SYNTHESIS, STORAGE, RELEASE ============ */
    subgraph cluster_synth {
        label = <<b>4 · PRL gene → granule → plasma: the fast and the slow branch separated</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fdf1e3"; color="#c07a1e"; fontcolor="#8a5410";
        PIT1         [label="Pit-1 (POU1F1)\nlineage TF", fillcolor="#fadfba"];
        ERA          [label="ERα at PRL distal\nenhancer", fillcolor="#fadfba"];
        PRLGENE      [label="PRL gene transcription", fillcolor="#fadfba", penwidth=2];
        PRLMRNA      [label="PRL mRNA pool\nτ ≈ days ← SLOW BRANCH", fillcolor="#fadfba", penwidth=2];
        PRLSYN       [label="PRL translation\n+ post-translational\nprocessing"];
        GRAN         [label="Secretory granule STORE\n(dense-core)", fillcolor="#fadfba", penwidth=2];
        EXO          [label="Ca²⁺-dependent EXOCYTOSIS\nτ ≈ minutes ← FAST BRANCH", fillcolor="#fadfba", penwidth=2];
        CRINO        [label="Crinophagy /\nintracellular degradation"];
        GLYCOS       [label="Glycosylation (G-PRL)\n— reduced bioactivity"];
        CATHD        [label="Cathepsin-D cleavage\n→ 16 kDa fragment"];
        RER          [label="RER + Golgi mass\n(cytoplasmic bulk)"];
    }

    /* ============ 5. TUMOUR CELL POPULATION DYNAMICS ============ */
    subgraph cluster_tumour {
        label = <<b>5 · Tumour dynamics: cell VOLUME (fast, reversible) vs cell NUMBER (slow, bankable)</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fbe9ec"; color="#b03050"; fontcolor="#7d1f38";
        LACTN        [label="NORMAL lactotroph pool\n(finite, feedback-intact)", fillcolor="#f7cfd8"];
        LACTS        [label="TUMOUR clone — D2R-SENSITIVE\n(cell number)", fillcolor="#f7cfd8", penwidth=2];
        LACTR        [label="TUMOUR clone — D2R-LOW / RESISTANT\n(cell number)", fillcolor="#f2b7c4", penwidth=2];
        CLONAL       [label="CLONAL SELECTION under therapy\nresistant fraction rises over YEARS", fillcolor="#f9d7d7", color="#b52b2b", penwidth=2];
        PROLIF       [label="Proliferation\n(Ki-67 / MIB-1 index)"];
        APOP         [label="Apoptosis"];
        CELLVOL      [label="MEAN CELL VOLUME\ngranule + RER bulk\n(τ ≈ 2-4 wk, REVERSIBLE)", fillcolor="#f7cfd8", penwidth=2];
        TVOL         [label="TUMOUR VOLUME (MRI)\n= N × cell volume + stroma", shape=doubleoctagon, fillcolor="#f2b7c4", color="#7d1f38", penwidth=2.4];
        FIBROS       [label="Perivascular FIBROSIS\nafter long-term DA\n(harder later surgery)", shape=octagon, fillcolor="#e0d6d6"];
        VEGFT        [label="VEGF-A / angiogenesis\nmicrovessel density"];
        NECRO        [label="Necrosis /\nhaemorrhagic infarction"];
        INVAS        [label="Cavernous sinus / dural\nINVASION (Knosp 3-4)", shape=octagon, fillcolor="#e0d6d6"];
        KI67         [label="Ki-67 ≥3% · p53 · mitoses\n→ aggressive PitNET"];
        CARCIN       [label="Pituitary CARCINOMA\n(CSF / systemic metastasis)", shape=octagon, fillcolor="#d9c3c3"];
    }

    /* ============ 6. GENETICS AND PATHOGENESIS ============ */
    subgraph cluster_genetics {
        label = <<b>6 · Pathogenesis: what makes a lactotroph clonal</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#f4ecf9"; color="#7a4fa8"; fontcolor="#54307a";
        SPORAD       [label="Sporadic monoclonal\nexpansion (~95%)", fillcolor="#e6d6f2"];
        SF3B1        [label="SF3B1 R625H hotspot\n(~20% of prolactinomas)\n→ aberrant ESRP2 splicing", fillcolor="#e6d6f2", penwidth=2];
        MEN1         [label="MEN1 (menin) loss\n— prolactinoma is the\ncommonest PitNET in MEN1", fillcolor="#e6d6f2"];
        AIP          [label="AIP mutation\n— young, large, invasive,\nDA-resistant", fillcolor="#e6d6f2", penwidth=2];
        MEN4         [label="CDKN1B (MEN4) ·\nDICER1 · GPR101 (X-LAG)"];
        HMGA2        [label="HMGA2 / 12q14-15 amp\n→ E2F1 activation"];
        MIRNA        [label="miR-93 / miR-183\n→ p21, PTEN loss"];
        ESTROGENP    [label="Oestrogen-driven lactotroph\nHYPERPLASIA → adenoma\n(rodent model, pregnancy)"];
        SEXDIM       [label="Sex dimorphism:\nF micro-dominant (20-50 y),\nM macro-invasive, later Dx", shape=note, fillcolor="#fbfbe8"];
    }

    /* ============ 7. OESTROGEN AND PREGNANCY ============ */
    subgraph cluster_estrogen {
        label = <<b>7 · The second driver: oestrogen (and why pregnancy is the stress test)</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#fdeaf4"; color="#b5418e"; fontcolor="#8a2c6b";
        E2P          [label="Oestradiol (E2)", fillcolor="#f9cfe6", penwidth=2];
        ERAUP        [label="ERα → PRL gene ↑\n+ D2R expression ↓", fillcolor="#f9cfe6"];
        LACTHYP      [label="Lactotroph HYPERPLASIA\n(normal pool expands\n~2x in pregnancy)"];
        PREG         [label="PREGNANCY\nE2 up to ~100x", shape=hexagon, fillcolor="#f9cfe6", penwidth=2];
        HPL          [label="Placental lactogen (hPL)\n+ decidual PRL"];
        POSTP        [label="Post-partum involution\n(occasionally curative)"];
        LACTATION    [label="Lactation\n(physiological, PRL 200-400)"];
        OCP          [label="Combined oral contraceptive /\noestrogen therapy", shape=hexagon, fillcolor="#f9cfe6"];
        PREGRISK     [label="Symptomatic enlargement\nin pregnancy:\nmicro ~2.7% · macro ~23%", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#f9cfe6"];
    }

    /* ============ 8. PLASMA PRL KINETICS AND MOLECULAR FORMS ============ */
    subgraph cluster_forms {
        label = <<b>8 · Plasma prolactin: four molecules, not one</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#e9f7f2"; color="#1e8f6e"; fontcolor="#136b52";
        PRLB         [label="MONOMERIC 23 kDa PRL\n— the BIOACTIVE pool\nt½ ≈ 25-50 min", fillcolor="#c6ecdf", penwidth=2.4];
        PRLDIM       [label="Big PRL (~50 kDa dimer)\npartially bioactive"];
        MACPRL       [label="BIG-BIG / MACROPROLACTIN\nPRL·IgG ~150 kDa\nSLOW clearance → accumulates", fillcolor="#c6ecdf", penwidth=2];
        APAB         [label="Anti-PRL autoantibody\n(IgG)", fillcolor="#c6ecdf"];
        GPRL         [label="Glycosylated PRL (G-PRL)\n~4x lower bioactivity"];
        PRL16        [label="16 kDa fragment\nANTI-angiogenic\n(different pharmacology)"];
        CLRREN       [label="Renal + hepatic clearance"];
        CKDPRL       [label="CKD: clearance ↓ +\ncentral dysregulation\n→ PRL 2-3x", fillcolor="#e6e6e6"];
        PULSAT       [label="Pulsatile secretion\n~14 pulses/day\n+ nocturnal surge"];
    }

    /* ============ 9. THE MEASUREMENT LAYER ============ */
    subgraph cluster_assay {
        label = <<b>9 · MEASUREMENT LAYER — where the number and the signal diverge (dashed = not biology)</b>>;
        style="rounded,dashed,filled"; fillcolor="#f2f2f2"; color="#555555"; fontcolor="#333333";
        node [style="rounded,dashed,filled", fillcolor="#fafafa"];
        ASSAY        [label="Two-site sandwich\nimmunoassay\ncapture · analyte · label", penwidth=2];
        BRIDGE       [label="Bridged complex ∝\n(A/K₁)/(1+A/K₁) × 1/(1+A/K₂)"];
        HOOK         [label="HOOK EFFECT\nvast antigen excess →\nsignal FALLS", shape=doubleoctagon, fillcolor="#e0e0e0", color="#222222", penwidth=2.4];
        FALSELOW     [label="Giant adenoma reported as\nPRL ~45 when true = 20 000\n→ misdiagnosed as NFPA", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#e0e0e0"];
        DILUTE       [label="1:100 DILUTION\n— the same curve 100-fold\ndown the analyte axis", shape=hexagon, fillcolor="#e0e0e0", penwidth=2];
        PEG          [label="PEG precipitation\nrecovery <40% ⇒ macroprolactin", shape=hexagon, fillcolor="#e0e0e0", penwidth=2];
        GELFIL       [label="Gel filtration chromatography\n(reference method)"];
        XREACT       [label="Platform cross-reactivity\nto macroprolactin\n(varies 0.3-1.2)"];
        PRLMEAS      [label="REPORTED PRL\n(what the clinician acts on)", shape=doubleoctagon, fillcolor="#d6d6d6", color="#222222", penwidth=2.4];
        FALSEHIGH    [label="Macroprolactinaemia:\nreported PRL high, axis INTACT\n→ treatment is futile", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#e0e0e0"];
        BIOASSAY     [label="Nb2 bioassay /\nBIOACTIVE prolactin"];
        VENIP        [label="Venipuncture stress /\nsingle sample artefact\n(cannulated series if <100)"];
    }

    /* ============ 10. PRL RECEPTOR SIGNALLING IN TARGETS ============ */
    subgraph cluster_prlr {
        label = <<b>10 · PRL receptor signalling at the targets</b>>;
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#eef7e9"; color="#4d8f2a"; fontcolor="#356b18";
        PRLR         [label="PRLR homodimer\n(long / intermediate /\nshort isoforms)", fillcolor="#d9edcc"];
        JAK2         [label="JAK2 → STAT5a/b", fillcolor="#d9edcc"];
        SOCS         [label="SOCS/CIS negative\nfeedback"];
        MAPKP        [label="Ras/MAPK + PI3K arms"];
        BREAST       [label="Mammary alveolar\nepithelium"];
        HYPOTAL      [label="Hypothalamic targets\n(KNDy, TIDA, appetite)", fillcolor="#d9edcc", penwidth=2];
        ADIPOSE      [label="Adipose / β-cell PRLR\n(metabolic arm)"];
        OSTEO        [label="Osteoblast PRLR\n(direct bone effect)"];
        IMMUNEP      [label="Lymphocyte PRLR\n(immunomodulation)"];
    }

